[2026-11-10 21:12:40] LLM suggestion: ## Analysis

### Pareto Front Assessment

Let me identify the non-dominated candidates (both objectives measured, good quality):

| Condition | Modulus (bar) | Pore Fraction | Quality notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| 19wt-5°C-0s-N2-1500s (run1) | 768 | 0.34 | Good quality |
| 20wt-5°C-150s-N2-1650s (run1) | 756 | 0.34 | Good quality |
| 17p5-5°C-550s-N2-1450s | 913 | 0.27 | Top-edge gap |
| 17p5-18°C-75s-N2-1800s (run1) | 937 | 0.27 | Minor wrinkles |
| 17p5-19°C-90s-N2-1800s (run1) | 860 | 0.27 | Wrinkle |
| 17p5-20°C-90s-N2-1250s (run2) | 961 | 0.27 | Good quality |
| 19wt-5°C-0s-N2-1500s (run4) | 890 | 0.29 | Minor wrinkles |
| 19wt-10°C-90s-N2-1800s | 1016 | 0.26 | Coverage gap + wrinkles |
| 18-15°C-600s-NoN2-1350s (run3) | 385 | 0.41 | Minor wrinkles |
| 17p5-19°C-90s-N2-1800s (run4) | 330 | 0.44 | Good quality |

**True Pareto-front candidates** (no other point dominates both):
- High modulus / lower pore fraction: ~960 bar / 0.27 (17p5-20°C-90s-N2-1250s run2)
- Balanced: ~768 bar / 0.34 (19wt-5°C-0s-N2-1500s run1), ~756 bar / 0.34 (20wt-5°C-150s-N2-1650s)
- High pore fraction / lower modulus: ~385 bar / 0.41 (18-15°C-600s-NoN2-1350s run3), ~330 bar / 0.44 (17p5-19°C-90s-N2-1800s run4)

### Validation Status Check

**17p5-20°C-90s-N2-1250s**: Three runs exist (run1: 673 bar/0.29, run2: 961 bar/0.27, run3: 385 bar/0.33). The spread is enormous (385–961 bar modulus), indicating high synthesis variability. Run2 is an extreme outlier with very high CV (SD=387 bar). This candidate is **not confirmed** — the three runs are inconsistent and the apparent high-modulus result is unreliable.

**19wt-5°C-0s-N2-1500s**: Run1 (768 bar/0.34, good quality) and runs 2–4 in the current campaign. Run3 (614/0.32, good quality) and run4 (890/0.29, minor wrinkles) are available. Run2 has no modulus measurement. The mean across run1, run3, run4 is roughly (768+614+890)/3 ≈ 757 bar with pore fractions 0.34, 0.32, 0.29 — showing some variability but consistently in the 600–900 bar range. This is a **promising balanced candidate** but pore fraction is variable (0.29–0.34).

**18-15°C-600s-NoN2-1350s**: Five runs. Modulus ranges 340–477 bar, pore fraction 0.36–0.41. This is the best high-pore-fraction candidate. Runs 3–5 show consistent pore fraction ~0.37–0.41 with modulus ~385–469 bar. Reasonably reproducible on pore fraction.

### Gap in the Pareto Front

The current Pareto front has a gap between:
- ~760 bar / 0.34 (19wt, 5°C bath)
- ~385 bar / 0.41 (18wt, 15°C bath, long wait, no N2)

A candidate achieving ~550–700 bar modulus with pore fraction ~0.37–0.40 would be a genuine Pareto improvement or clarification. The high-pore-fraction region (0.38–0.42) with moderate modulus (>450 bar) is underexplored.

### What drives high pore fraction?

From the data:
- Higher bath temperature (15–21°C) → higher pore fraction (0.35–0.44)
- No nitrogen → slightly higher pore fraction in some cases
- Lower polymer concentration (17–17.5 wt%) → higher pore fraction
- Longer coupon-to-bath wait → higher pore fraction (skin formation)
- The 18-15°C-600s-NoN2 series achieves 0.37–0.41 pore fraction

### What drives high modulus?

- Higher polymer concentration (19–20 wt%) → higher modulus
- Cold bath (5°C) → faster phase separation, denser structure
- Moderate wait time

### Proposed Experiment

To explore the gap between the two Pareto extremes, I want a condition that could achieve **moderate-to-high modulus (>500 bar) with high pore fraction (>0.37)**. 

The 18wt% polymer at bath_temp=15°C with no N2 and long wait achieves high pore fraction but low modulus. What if I increase polymer concentration slightly to 18.5 wt% while keeping the high-pore-fraction conditions (bath_temp=15°C, no N2, moderate-long wait)?

The 18.5wt% at bath_temp=5°C (run2) gave 536 bar / 0.28 — cold bath suppresses pore fraction. At bath_temp=14°C (run1) gave 581 bar / 0.33 — promising but only one measurement. 

A new candidate: **18.5 wt%, bath_temp=15°C, no N2, moderate wait (~200s), mixing_temp=65°C, pullcast_speed=10, nips_bath_wait=1800s**. This is distinct from existing experiments (18.5wt% has only been tested at bath_temp=5°C and 12°C and 14°C with N2). Bath_temp=15°C + no N2 + 18.5wt% is unexplored. The hypothesis: higher polymer concentration than the 17.5wt% high-pore-fraction experiments should boost modulus, while the warm bath + no N2 + moderate wait should maintain elevated pore fraction.

This is a genuine exploration point in an under-sampled region of the Pareto front.

```json
{
  "mixing_temp": 65,
  "bath_temp": 15,
  "pullcast_speed": 10,
  "nitrogen": false,
  "coupon_to_bath_wait_time": 200,
  "nips_bath_wait_time": 1800,
  "polymer_wt": 18.5,
  "additive_wt": 0.0
}
```

**Rationale:** This experiment targets the unexplored gap in the Pareto front between high-modulus/low-pore-fraction and low-modulus/high-pore-fraction regimes. Increasing polymer_wt from 17.5 to 18.5 wt% (relative to the high-pore-fraction 17.5wt% series) should increase modulus, while bath_temp=15°C + no N2 + 200s wait (conditions associated with elevated pore fraction in the 17.5wt% and 18wt% series) should maintain pore fraction above 0.35. This combination has not been tested. If successful, it would represent a Pareto improvement over the 18wt%/15°C/no-N2 series (higher modulus at similar pore fraction) and over the 18.5wt%/5°C series (higher pore fraction at similar modulus).

