**Approximate Pareto front** (neither dominated):
- High modulus / lower pore fraction: ~960 bar / 0.27 (17p5-20deg-90s-N2-1250s run2)
- Balanced: ~767 bar / 0.34 (19-5deg-0s-N2-1500s run1); ~755 bar / 0.34 (20-5deg-150s-N2-1650s run1)
- High pore fraction / lower modulus: ~330 bar / 0.44 (17p5-19deg-90s-N2-1800s run4)

### Validation Status Check

**17p5-20deg-90s-N2-1250s** (mixing_temp=50, bath_temp=20, pullcast_speed=7, N2=true, wait=90s, nips=1250s, polymer=17.5):
- Run1: 672.84 bar / 0.29 PF — good quality
- Run2: 960.52 bar / 0.27 PF — good quality (large apparent gain, high SD=387 bar)
- Run3: 385.37 bar / 0.33 PF — good quality

Three runs exist. The mean across runs is roughly (673+961+385)/3 ≈ 673 bar, with enormous run-to-run variability (SD ~290 bar across syntheses). Run2's 960 bar is an outlier. The condition is **not confirmed** as a reliable high-modulus performer — the inter-synthesis variability is very high and the three runs span 385–961 bar.

**19-5deg-0s-N2-1500s** (mixing_temp=60, bath_temp=5, pullcast_speed=3, N2=true, wait=0s, nips=1500s, polymer=19.0):
- Run1: 767.83 bar / 0.34 PF — good quality
- Run2: missing modulus/PF
- Run3: 614.09 bar / 0.32 PF — wrinkles
- Run4: 890.19 bar / 0.29 PF — minor wrinkles, high CV=0.22, SD=251 bar

This condition shows high modulus but also high variability and wrinkling issues. Run1 remains the cleanest result at 767/0.34.

**The balanced Pareto region (moderate-high modulus ~700–800 bar AND moderate-high pore fraction ~0.32–0.35) is under-explored.** Most high-modulus candidates have pore fraction ≤0.29, and most high-pore-fraction candidates have modulus ≤500 bar.

### Gap Identification

Looking at the data pattern:
- **Bath temp 5°C + polymer 19 wt%** gives high modulus but variable pore fraction (0.29–0.34)
- **Bath temp 15–19°C + polymer 17.5 wt% + no N2 + longer wait** gives higher pore fraction (0.35–0.44) but lower modulus (330–520 bar)
- **The intermediate zone** — bath temp ~8–12°C, polymer ~18–18.5 wt%, moderate wait time, N2 — is partially explored but mostly with wrinkle issues

The condition **18-12deg-90s-N2-1800s** (run1) gave 580.71 bar / 0.28 PF with good quality. Run2 and run3 had coverage/wrinkle issues. This is not a strong Pareto candidate.

**An under-sampled promising region**: polymer_wt ~18.5–19 wt%, bath_temp ~7–9°C, moderate pullcast speed ~8–10, N2=true, short wait ~30–60s. This region could combine the higher modulus from higher polymer concentration with slightly more pore fraction than the 5°C bath. The 5°C bath with 19 wt% gives high modulus but pore fraction only 0.29–0.34. A slightly warmer bath (7–9°C) with 18.5–19 wt% might shift pore fraction upward while maintaining reasonable modulus.

**Also notable**: The 19-5deg-0s-N2-1500s run1 (767 bar / 0.34 PF) is a strong balanced Pareto candidate but has only one clean measurement (run2 missing, run3 wrinkled, run4 high CV). This warrants a validation repeat. However, run4 at 890 bar / 0.29 PF shows the condition can produce high modulus but with lower pore fraction and high variability.

### Decision

The **19-5deg-0s-N2-1500s** condition (run1: 767 bar / 0.34 PF) is a strong balanced Pareto candidate. Run1 is the only clean, complete measurement. Run2 has no modulus/PF data. Run3 has wrinkles. Run4 has high CV and lower PF. This condition has not been independently reproduced with both objectives confirmed at comparable quality. **Validation is warranted.**

The original parameters: mixing_temp=60, bath_temp=5, pullcast_speed=3, nitrogen=true, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=0, nips_bath_wait_time=1500, polymer_wt=19.0, additive_wt=0.0. All within bounds. This is feasible.

**Expected benefit**: Confirm whether 767 bar / 0.34 PF is reproducible. If confirmed, this is a strong balanced Pareto point — higher pore fraction than most high-modulus candidates (which cluster at 0.27–0.29) while maintaining competitive modulus. This would establish a reliable anchor on the balanced portion of the Pareto front.

